# A root .pubignore DISABLES pub's git-based file listing entirely — .gitignore
# stops being consulted. So everything .gitignore excludes has to be repeated
# here, or it ships. (A single `flutter test` run leaves a 44 MB .dill under
# build/, which is exactly how this was caught.)
#
# The dot-prefixed entries below are belt-and-braces: measured in #42, pub skips
# dot-directories on its own — a planted `.claude/worktrees/probe/junk.txt` did
# not reach the archive, while a control `stray_control.txt` in the same run
# did. They stay because the rule above is worth keeping mechanically checkable
# (`.pubignore` ⊇ `.gitignore`), and because that skip is pub's behaviour rather
# than a promise. The entries that actually do the work are the non-dot ones —
# build/, node_modules/, coverage/, migrate_working_dir/, *.log.

# --- mirrors .gitignore ---
*.class
*.log
*.pyc
*.swp
.DS_Store
.atom/
.buildlog/
.history
.svn/
migrate_working_dir/

*.iml
*.ipr
*.iws
.idea/

/pubspec.lock
**/doc/api/
.dart_tool/
.flutter-plugins-dependencies
/build/
/coverage/

node_modules/
.playwright-mcp/
.claude/worktrees/

# --- tracked in git, but not published ---
docs/
.github/
tool/

# The whole suite is excluded, not a growing list of files.
#
# Most of these tests read something: parity fixtures (hundreds of KB per
# supported upstream version) or golden images (25 KB each). None of that data
# belongs in a consumer's archive, and a test shipped without the data it reads
# is a suite that cannot pass. Excluding the pair file-by-file worked twice and
# then grew again on the third ticket, so the rule replaces the list.
#
# Nothing is lost: pub.dev does not run package tests, and the suite is one
# `git clone` away.
test/

# The same reasoning, one directory further out. `example/` itself **is**
# published — pana scores it, and it is the answer to "show me this in an app".
# Its integration test is not that: it is #110's harness, which drives a real
# engine to assert that the buffer lands on whole device pixels. A consumer has
# no reason to run it and pub.dev does not.
#
# It ships by default because the rule above stops at the root `test/`, and this
# directory did not exist when that rule was written. Nothing catches it —
# `pub publish --dry-run` reports **0 warnings** either way, because no gate
# knows what belongs in an archive. Read the file list; that is the check.
example/integration_test/

# Same rule, same reason: a spike is an experiment, not a demonstration. This one
# is #117's — it exists to find what a compositing layer at a fractional origin
# resamples, and it is deleted once that is answered. Shipping it would put a
# diagnostic screen in the archive that no consumer can act on.
example/spike/

CLAUDE.md
test.md
